HTB — Scalped Platform
Category	Difficulty	Platform	Date compiled
Hardware / Embedded forensics	Very Easy	Hack The Box	2026

Skills demonstrated: U-Boot console use, I²C bus enumeration, EEPROM forensics, internal address-width troubleshooting, AES-256-CBC decryption.

Summary

The challenge exposes the U-Boot console of a satellite on-board computer (OBC) that can no longer boot. Two EEPROMs survive on separate I²C buses. The bus 1 EEPROM holds a corrupted backup U-Boot environment containing an encrypted_flag variable. The bus 0 EEPROM appears erased when read with two-byte internal addressing. Switching to one-byte addressing (i2c olen 0x50 1) exposes an AES-256-CBC key and IV stored in plaintext, which decrypt the flag.

Challenge description

A satellite On-Board Computer (OBC) was recovered from a damaged flatsat, but critical components are missing and the system can no longer complete its boot sequence. While the hardware appears unusable, traces of its previous configuration may still remain. Investigate the abandoned onboard computer and recover what was left behind.

Tools
nc — connecting to the U-Boot console
U-Boot i2c commands — bus selection, probing, offset-length configuration, memory dumps
OpenSSL — AES-256-CBC decryption
Walkthrough
1. Initial reconnaissance
bash
nc -nv <TARGET_IP> <PORT>

The boot log shows the environment failing to load from either EEPROM:

Loading Environment from EEPROM...
*** Warning - EEPROM erased, fallback to other I2C bus

Loading Environment from EEPROM...
*** Warning - bad CRC, using default environment

U-Boot also reports no MMC card, SPI flash or USB device, then drops to an interactive prompt (=>).

With no boot media present, the task is to inspect the surviving non-volatile storage, not to restore the operating system. The two warnings point to two EEPROMs: one reported as erased, and one with a corrupted environment.

2. I²C enumeration
=> i2c bus
=> i2c dev 1
=> i2c probe
Valid chip addresses: 18 50 68
Address	Likely device	Basis
0x18	LIS3DH accelerometer	Default LIS3DH address, plus register inspection
0x50	EEPROM (24C-series)	Standard EEPROM base address, referenced by the boot errors
0x68	Real-time clock	Common RTC address (for example DS1307 or DS3231)

The EEPROM at 0x50 is the primary target, because both boot warnings reference loading the environment from EEPROM.

3. Backup environment (bus 1, offset 0x800)
=> i2c dev 1
=> i2c md 0x50 0x0800.2 0x100

The dump contains readable U-Boot environment variables:

board=flat-cubesat-obc1
boot_targets=mmc1
cpu=XC7Z010
encrypted_flag=ed38f62b6ad2f6375123848dca458e4bf2982e33829cef861ad0bb3a0b7860c9

The encrypted_flag value is 32 bytes, which is consistent with two AES blocks. Its length alone does not identify the algorithm. The rest of the environment contained no obvious key material.

The ciphertext can also be read on its own at offset 0x880:

=> i2c md 0x50 0x0880.2 0x80
4. Key material (bus 0, one-byte addressing)

Reading the bus 0 EEPROM with two-byte internal addressing returned only 0xFF, which appeared to confirm the "EEPROM erased" warning:

=> i2c dev 0
=> i2c md 0x50 0x0000.2 0x80

The chip, however, expects one-byte internal addressing. Setting the offset length to 1 and reading at 0x100 exposed configuration strings that had survived the apparent wipe:

=> i2c dev 0
=> i2c olen 0x50 1
=> i2c md 0x50 0x100 0x80
AES_KEY=a07e99c0…[REDACTED]…d0cbe89c
IV=813b320e…[REDACTED]
AES_MODE=CBC

The key is 32 bytes (AES-256) and the IV is 16 bytes. The values are truncated here so that the write-up does not publish the means to decrypt the flag.

i2c olen changes only how U-Boot addresses the chip during the session. It does not write to the EEPROM, so the investigation remained read-only.

5. Decryption
bash
echo -n '<ENCRYPTED_FLAG_HEX>' \
  | xxd -r -p \
  | openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -d \
      -K <AES_KEY_HEX> \
      -iv <IV_HEX>

If xxd is unavailable, python3 -c "import sys; sys.stdout.buffer.write(bytes.fromhex(sys.argv[1]))" <hex> produces the same raw bytes.

OpenSSL removes the PKCS#7 padding by default. The output is a 26-byte plaintext in HTB{...} format.

6. Flag
HTB{REDACTED}
Root cause

Two compounding failures:

Incomplete sanitization of persistent storage (CWE-1330, Remanent Data Readable after Memory Erase). The bus 0 EEPROM was not actually erased. Data survived outside the region that the default read covered.
Encryption key stored in plaintext beside the ciphertext (CWE-312, Cleartext Storage of Sensitive Information; CWE-321, Use of Hard-coded Cryptographic Key). Anyone with console access to both EEPROMs holds everything needed to decrypt, so the encryption added no protection.

An exposed, unauthenticated bootloader console (CWE-1191, On-Chip Debug and Test Interface with Improper Access Control) made both reachable.

Defensive takeaways
Protect key material in hardware: use a secure element, the SoC's eFuse/OTP key storage or a TPM rather than plain EEPROM, and never store a key on the same accessible medium as the data it protects.
Verify erasure across the full address space: write and read back every byte at the device's correct address width. A read returning 0xFF under the wrong addressing mode does not prove a wipe.
Lock down the bootloader: disable or password-protect the U-Boot console in production (for example with CONFIG_AUTOBOOT_KEYED and a non-interactive bootdelay), and restrict debug interfaces.
Authenticate the environment: use a read-only or signed U-Boot environment so that a corrupted or tampered one is rejected rather than inspected.
Key lessons
A device that cannot boot can still give up its data. Boot failure is not data destruction.
Check EEPROM addressing assumptions. The wrong internal address width can make retained data look erased.
A CRC failure means the environment is unusable as a whole, not that its contents are gone.
Read-only inspection first: everything needed came from U-Boot's i2c read commands without modifying storage.
